Purpose: Document all mismatches found and their causes

[49m[38;5;10m+      1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # Mismatches found while verifying the translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every entry below is a difference that was observed between
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/build/driver` and `translation/target/release/driver` on the same stdin,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m together with what caused it and what changed in the Rust code. The C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m was never modified; where it does something surprising, the Rust program was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m changed to do the same thing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m How the comparison was done: build both, feed both the same bytes on stdin, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m compare stdout, stderr and the wait status. Roughly 2,250 generated inputs were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m used during the hunt, plus 4,007 inputs aimed only at the `scanf` layer and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2,014,848 float values aimed only at the `printf` layer.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 1. `stb_perlin_noise3_wrap_nonpow2` reads far outside the tables
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** 115 of 694 inputs with `which == 5` disagreed. Example:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in : 5 900.5 900.25 900.125 1000 1000 1000 0 0 0 0 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C  : 0.146484375
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: 0                       (before the fix)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Unlike `stb_perlin_noise3_internal`, which masks every coordinate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with `& 255` and therefore can only produce indices `0..=510`, the non-power-of-
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m two variant computes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int x0 = px % x_wrap2, ...
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m r0 = stb__perlin_randtab[x0];
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ...
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m r00 = stb__perlin_randtab[r0+y0];
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m n000 = stb__perlin_grad(stb__perlin_randtab_grad_idx[r00+z0], ...);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `x0`, `y0` and `z0` are bounded only by the caller's `*_wrap` arguments, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m indices routinely run off the ends of the 512-byte tables, and the resulting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `grad_idx` (an arbitrary `unsigned char` rather than one of the table's `0..=11`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m values) then indexes `basis[12][4]` out of range too. The C program reads
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whatever the linker placed next.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The translation had been written to return `0` for any out-of-range index. That
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is a reasonable thing to do and it is *not* what the C does.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `src/memimage.rs` embeds the byte image of the mapped range
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `[0x400000, 0x406000)` of the compiled C program, and `src/perlin.rs` reads the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tables out of that image using the same address arithmetic the compiler emits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`base + (i64)(int)index`). The driver is a **non-PIE** executable, so these
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m addresses are fixed and the reads are reproducible. `noise3_internal` keeps
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m using the plain transcribed tables, with a `debug_assert!` recording that its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m indices are provably in range.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 2. Out-of-range indices that are far enough out kill the C process
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in : 5 4032 0.25 0.125 4033 0 0 0 0 0 0 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C  : no output, killed by SIGSEGV
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: 0                       (before the fix)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The writable segment mapping ends at `0x406000` and the read-only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m segments start at `0x400000`, so `stb__perlin_randtab[i]` is readable exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m for `i` in `-20544 ..= 4031`. Anything outside faults. Boundaries were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m confirmed by probing the C program: index `4031` prints a value, `4032` faults;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-20544` prints a value, `-20545` faults.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `src/trap.rs::sigsegv()` provokes a genuine `SIGSEGV` (a volatile read
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m through a `black_box`-hidden bad pointer) rather than calling `exit`. Death by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m signal and `exit(139)` are different wait statuses, and the tests compare the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m wait status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 3. `INT_MIN % -1` raises `SIGFPE`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in : 5 -2147483648 0.5 0.5 -1 1 1 0 0 0 0 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C  : no output, killed by SIGFPE (status 136)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: 0                       (before the fix)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** `px % x_wrap2` becomes `idiv`, which raises `#DE` when the *quotient*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is unrepresentable — `-INT_MIN` overflows — even though the remainder would be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0`. Rust's `%` panics on that input and `wrapping_rem` quietly returns `0`;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m neither matches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `px == INT_MIN` is reachable because `stb__perlin_fastfloor` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cvttss2si`, which yields `INT_MIN` for NaN and for anything out of range, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m because `x = -2147483648` floors to exactly `INT_MIN`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `perlin::crem` checks for `INT_MIN % -1` (and for a zero divisor) and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m calls `trap::sigfpe()`, which executes a real trapping `idiv`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 4. NaN sign: the final `addss` in `stb__perlin_grad` has its operands swapped
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Nine of the remaining mismatches were a NaN sign flip, e.g.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in : 0 -31 -nan NaN 1187515947 -5 -17 -200 1.23458773e+19 0.824976073 67.516731 2
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C  : nan
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: -nan                    (before the fix)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** `printf("%.9g")` prints `-nan` for a NaN with the sign bit set, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NaN *sign* is observable, and on x86 `addss`/`subss`/`mulss` return the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m source operand when it is a NaN and only otherwise the second. The translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m already modelled that, but it had the operand order of one expression wrong.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m For `grad[0]*x + grad[1]*y + grad[2]*z`, gcc keeps the running sum in `xmm1`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m computes `grad[2]*z` into `xmm0`, and emits `addss xmm0, xmm1` — the *product*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is the first source of the final add, not the partial sum:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```asm
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mulss  xmm1, [rbp-0x18]   ; grad[0]*x
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mulss  xmm0, [rbp-0x1c]   ; grad[1]*y
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m addss  xmm1, xmm0         ; fadd(g0*x, g1*y)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mulss  xmm0, [rbp-0x20]   ; grad[2]*z
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m addss  xmm0, xmm1         ; fadd(g2*z, partial)   <-- this way round
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `perlin::grad_dot` now computes `fadd(fmul(g2, z), partial)`. The same
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reading of the disassembly also corrected the accumulation order in all three
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fractal functions, which are `fadd(<this octave>, sum)` and not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fadd(sum, <this octave>)`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```asm
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mulss  xmm0, [rbp-0xc]    ; noise * amplitude
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m movss  xmm1, [rbp-0x10]   ; sum
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m addss  xmm0, xmm1         ; fadd(noise*amplitude, sum)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and the operand order inside the `stb__perlin_ease` macro, where gcc puts the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m literal first (`mulss xmm0(=6), xmm1(=a)`, `addss xmm0(=10), xmm1`). The ease
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m change is cosmetic — only one operand there can ever be a NaN — but it is now
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m faithful.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stb__perlin_lerp` was already correct: `subss xmm0(=b), a`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `mulss xmm0, t`, `addss xmm0, a`, i.e. `fadd(fmul(fsub(b, a), t), a)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 5. `(float) fabs(r)` is a sign-bit clear, not a `double` round trip
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Included in the NaN-sign group above, e.g.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in : 4 nan 0.25 0.125 0 0 0 0 2 0.5 0 1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The translation implemented `(float) fabs(r)` literally, as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `((r as f64).abs()) as f32`. gcc folds the whole thing into one instruction:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```asm
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m movss  xmm1, [rip+...]    ; 0x7fffffff
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m andps  xmm1, xmm0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The `f64` round trip differs from a bit-and for NaN payloads (`cvtsd2ss` quiets
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a signalling NaN), which is not visible through `%g`, but writing it as a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bit-and is exactly right and cheaper.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `perlin::fabsf` is `f32::from_bits(r.to_bits() & 0x7fff_ffff)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 6. Three slots in the process image that the file does not contain
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** After fixes 1–5, three inputs still disagreed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in : 5 0.030039715 -199.998690 -122.666708 256 -227 8 39 260.068491 0x1p1000 NaN 2
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C  : 0.123439044      Rust: 0.123439014
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in : 5 3.9209065e+19 215 -0.364172032 -61 -47 157 256 -8.06055017e+19 0x1p4 1.38143752 -1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C  : inf              Rust: -nan
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in : 5 0.873111399 -0.237581491 -72 -155 -256 -279 72 -277.855425 -79.630795 -nan 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C  : 0                Rust: killed by SIGSEGV
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The image had been reconstructed from the ELF file, but the dynamic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m linker overwrites several slots before `main` runs, and negative table indices
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m land on them. The affected bytes are the `.dynamic` `DT_DEBUG` pointer, the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `.got` entry for `__libc_start_main`, `.got.plt[1..2]` (the `link_map` pointer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `_dl_runtime_resolve`), and the `.got.plt` slot for `__isoc99_scanf` — the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m last of which is resolved because `scanf` is called *before* the noise code
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs, while the `printf` slot is still the unresolved PLT stub and so does match
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the file.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** The constant bytes of those pointers were recovered from the running C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program and baked into the image (see "how the image was checked" below). They
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cross-check against the symbol tables of the system libraries: the recovered
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m low bytes of `&__libc_start_main`, `&__isoc99_scanf`, `&_r_debug` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `&_dl_runtime_resolve_xsavec` are `0x40 0xa6`, `0x60 0x53`, `0x58` and `0x70`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matching `readelf -s` offsets `0x2a640`, `0x55360`, `0x39358` and `0x14d70`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## The one thing that cannot be matched
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 19 bytes of the image carry ASLR entropy — the middle bytes of the four
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m relocated pointers listed in item 6. In `randtab` index terms:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | index | address | contents |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    202[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    203[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-383 ..= -379` | `0x404ec1..=0x404ec5` | `.dynamic` `DT_DEBUG` → `&_r_debug` in `ld.so` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    204[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-118 ..= -116` | `0x404fca..=0x404fcc` | `.got` → `&__libc_start_main` in libc |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    205[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-79 ..= -76` | `0x404ff1..=0x404ff4` | `.got.plt[1]` → `struct link_map *` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    206[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-71 ..= -68` | `0x404ff9..=0x404ffc` | `.got.plt[2]` → `&_dl_runtime_resolve` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    207[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-54 ..= -52` | `0x40500a..=0x40500c` | `.got.plt` → resolved `&__isoc99_scanf` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    208[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    209[0m:[38;5;10m[49m For the handful of inputs whose table indices reach those bytes, **the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    210[0m:[38;5;10m[49m does not agree with itself**. For instance
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    211[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `5 -100.5 -100.25 -100.125 -256 -256 -256 0 0 0 0 0` printed `0.330785155`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    212[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0.220523417` and `0` on three consecutive runs and died of `SIGSEGV` on a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    213[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fourth, because a garbage `grad_idx` sometimes points past the end of the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    214[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mapping. No translation can reproduce a program whose output is a function of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    215[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the address-space layout, so the test suite does not assert on those inputs;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    216[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/differential.rs` keeps the sweep's `which == 5` wrap values
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    217[0m:[38;5;10m[49m non-negative, which makes every table index non-negative and therefore keeps
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    218[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the sweep away from the region entirely.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    219[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    220[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Out of 24,576 image bytes, these 19 are the only ones that differ from what the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    221[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C process actually reads.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    222[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    223[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    224[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    225[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## How the image was checked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    226[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    227[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C program was turned into an oracle for its own memory. For
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    228[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    229[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    230[0m:[38;5;10m[49m which=5, x=<n>, y=0.25, z=0.125, x_wrap=<n+1>, y_wrap=z_wrap=0, seed=<s>
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    231[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    232[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    233[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the only unusual read the driver performs is `stb__perlin_randtab[n]`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    234[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`n % (n+1) == n` and `(n+1) % (n+1) == 0`, and the same holds for `x = -1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    235[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with `x_wrap = n+1` when `n <= -3`), and the printed value is a function of that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    236[0m:[38;5;10m[49m single byte. Tabulating the model over all 256 candidate bytes and matching the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    237[0m:[38;5;10m[49m driver's actual output recovers the byte the C process really saw; repeating with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    238[0m:[38;5;10m[49m several `seed` values resolves the few collisions and repeating each probe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    239[0m:[38;5;10m[49m identifies the bytes that change between runs.
