[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    252[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   overflow, whitespace variations, random token soup) produced the same twelve
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    253[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   values and the same return count.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    254[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    255[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Things that were already right
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    256[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    257[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Worth recording, because they are the parts a translation usually gets wrong and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    258[0m:[38;5;10m[49m these did not need changing:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    259[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    260[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `scanf` skipping newlines, so one call reads fields spread over many lines,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    261[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and stopping at the first matching failure with every later variable left at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    262[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   its zero initialiser.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    263[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * glibc's `%d` being `(int) strtol(...)`, so `12345678901234567890` saturates to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    264[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `LONG_MAX` and truncates to `-1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    265[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * glibc's `%f` not being `strtof`: a partially matched `inf`/`nan`/`0x` is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    266[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   matching failure rather than a shorter match, and a stray exponent marker is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    267[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   swallowed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    268[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `stb__perlin_fastfloor` using `cvttss2si` semantics (`INT_MIN` for NaN and for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    269[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   out-of-range values) and the `a < ai` comparison being false for NaN.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    270[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `(unsigned char) seed` and `(unsigned char) i` truncation.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    271[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `printf("%.9g")` round-half-to-even, the two-digit minimum exponent field, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    272[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the `nan`/`-nan`/`inf`/`-inf` spellings.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    273[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    274[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Portability of the memory image
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    275[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    276[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `src/memimage.rs` describes one particular build of the C program: gcc 11.5.0,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    277[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cmake ..` with no build type, non-PIE, glibc 2.34+. The parts of it that the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    278[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program reads *in bounds* — the two tables and `basis` — are fixed by the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    279[0m:[38;5;10m[49m source. The parts it reads *out of bounds* are a property of that binary's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    280[0m:[38;5;10m[49m layout, and a different compiler or link would move them. There is no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    281[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input-independent way around this: the behaviour being reproduced is undefined
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    282[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in C, and its observable result is whatever the linker did.
